.\" groff -man -Tascii pwsafe-cli.1
.\"
.TH PWSAFE-CLI 1 "OCT 2026" Linux "User Manuals"
.SH NAME
pwsafe-cli \- Command-line interface for Password Safe
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pwsafe-cli safe --imp[=file] --text|--xml
.br
.B pwsafe-cli safe --exp[=file] --text|--xml
.br
.B pwsafe-cli safe --create
.br
.B pwsafe-cli safe --add=field1=value1,field2=value2,...
.br
.B pwsafe-cli safe --search=<text>
.RI [ --ignore-case ]
.RI [ --subset=<Field><OP><string>[/iI] ]
.RI [ --fields=f1,f2,... ]
.RI [ --delete | --update=Field1=Value1,Field2=Value2,... | --print[=field1,field2,...] ]
.RI [ --yes ]
.RI [ --generate-totp ]
.br
.B pwsafe-cli safe --diff=<other-safe>
.RI [ --subset=<Field><OP><Value>[/iI] ]
.RI [ --fields=f1,f2,... ]
.RI [ --unified | --context | --sidebyside ]
.RI [ --colwidth=column-size ]
.br
.B pwsafe-cli safe --synchronize=<other-safe>
.RI [ --subset=<Field><OP><string>[/iI] ]
.RI [ --fields=f1,f2,... ]
.RI [ --dry-run ]
.br
.B pwsafe-cli safe --merge=<other-safe>
.RI [ --subset=<Field><OP><Value>[/iI] ]
.RI [ --dry-run ]
.br
.B pwsafe-cli --help[=add|create|delete|diff|merge|search|sync|synchronize|update]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pwsafe-cli
is the command-line interface for Password Safe (pwsafe). It can create and modify
.B pwsafe 
safes (password databases), import and export entries, search and filter entries,
compare, synchronize, and merge two safes.
.PP
The program prompts for master passphrases as required. The
.B --passphrase
.I passphrase
and
.B --passphrase2
.I passphrase
options may be used to skip the corresponding prompt, but using them should
be avoided if possible for security reasons.
.SH OPTIONS
.IP "--imp[=file]"
Import a safe from
.I file
and represent the imported data as text or XML, as selected by
.B --text
or
.B --xml
\&.
.IP "--exp[=file]"
Export a safe to
.I file
in text or XML format, as selected by
.B --text
or
.B --xml
\&.
.IP "--text"
Select text format for import or export.
.IP "--xml"
Select XML format for import or export.
.IP "--create"
Create a new safe.
.IP "--add=field1=value1,field2=value2,..."
Add an entry containing the specified field/value pairs.
.IP "--search=<text>"
Search for entries containing
.I text
\&. Additional options can restrict the search, select fields, print matching
entries, update them, delete them, or generate a TOTP code.
.IP "--ignore-case"
Ignore case when performing a search.
.IP "--subset=<Field><OP><string>[/iI]"
Restrict the operation to entries matching the specified field expression.
The comparison operator
.I OP
is described in the
.B SUBSET EXPRESSIONS
section.
A trailing
.B /i
requests case-insensitive comparison; a trailing
.B /I
requests case-sensitive comparison.
For diff, merge and synchronize, subset matching is currently case-insensitive:
these operations do not apply the parsed case modifier.
.IP "--fields=f1,f2,..."
Select the fields to be considered or displayed by the operation, as applicable.
.IP "--delete"
Delete entries selected by the search or subset expression.
.IP "--update=Field1=Value1,Field2=Value2,..."
Update the selected entries with the specified field/value pairs.
.IP "--print[=field1,field2,...]"
Print matching entries. If fields are supplied, print the specified fields only.
.IP "--yes"
Confirm search-based modifications without prompting. Merge and synchronize
already save without a confirmation prompt; this option has no effect on them.
.IP "--generate-totp"
Generate the TOTP value for the selected entry.
.IP "--diff=<other-safe>"
Compare the current safe (left) with
.I other-safe
(right) without saving either safe. Entries match by group, title and username,
not UUID. Reports unique entries and differences in matching entries.
The output format may be selected with
.B --unified
,
.B --context
, or
.B --sidebyside
\&. Select comparison fields with
.B --fields
(default: all supported fields); Record Modified Time is excluded.
Output can contain plaintext passwords, password history and other secrets.
Successful comparison returns 0 even when differences are found, unlike the
system diff command.
.IP "--unified"
Display differences in unified format (the default). Minus identifies the first
safe and plus identifies the other safe.
.IP "--context"
Display differences in context format.
.IP "--sidebyside"
Display differences side by side.
.IP "--colwidth=column-size"
Set the width of each side-by-side diff column (default: 60).
.IP "--synchronize=<other-safe>"
Copy selected fields one-way from
.I other-safe
(source) into matching entries in the first safe (destination).
Entries match by group, title and username, not UUID.
Source-only entries are not added; destination-only entries are not removed.
The source is unchanged. Source values overwrite destination values even if
older or empty; no newest-version selection or conflict copies are provided.
Protected destination entries and source aliases/shortcuts are skipped.
Restrict source entries with
.B --subset
and copied fields with
.B --fields
(default: all eligible fields). UUID and attachment-reference identity fields
are excluded. Back up the destination first: changes are saved without asking.
.B --help=sync
and
.B --help=synchronize
show the same help; the operation flag is
.B --synchronize
\&.
.IP "--merge=<other-safe>"
Merge entries from
.I other-safe
into the first safe, leaving the source unchanged.
Entries match by group, title and username, not UUID.
Source-only entries are added and destination-only entries are kept.
If a matching entry differs in merge's predefined comparison fields, the source
entry is added with a title suffixed with -merged-<timestamp>; the destination
entry is not overwritten. Matching entries without such differences are skipped.
Not every stored field is compared; creation/modification timestamps alone do
not create conflict copies. Aliases and shortcuts are handled with base entries.
Merge neither chooses the newest version nor propagates deletions.
Restrict source entries with
.B --subset
\&;
.B --fields
does not affect merge. Back up the destination first: changes are saved without
asking.
.IP "--dry-run"
For merge and synchronize, perform the operation in memory but do not save the
destination. This does not print a preview or change/conflict report.
.IP "--passphrase=<passphrase>"
Supply the master passphrase without prompting.
For security reasons, this should be avoided if possible.
.IP "--passphrase2=<2nd passphrase>"
Supply the second master passphrase without prompting.
For security reasons, this should be avoided if possible.
.IP "--help[=add|create|delete|diff|merge|search|sync|synchronize|update]"
Display general help or help for the specified operation.
.SH SUBSET EXPRESSIONS
Subset expressions have the form
.B <Field><OP><Value>
with an optional trailing
.B /i
or
.B /I
modifier.
.PP
The supported operators are:
.IP "=="
Match exactly.
.IP "!=="
Negated exact match.
.IP "^="
Match values beginning with the specified value.
.IP "!^="
Negated beginning-with match.
.IP "$="
Match values ending with the specified value.
.IP "!$="
Negated ending-with match.
.IP "~="
Match values containing the specified value.
.IP "!~="
Negated contains match.
.PP
The
.B !
character is the negation operator.
A trailing
.B /i
requests case-insensitive comparison, while
.B /I
requests case-sensitive comparison.
.SH FIELD NAMES
The following field names are valid:
.PP
.nf
Group/Title
Username
Password
URL
AutoType
Created Time
Password Modified Time
Last Access Time
Password Expiry Date
Record Modified Time
History
Notes
Group
Title
Password Policy
Password Expiry Interval
Run Command
DCA
e-mail
Protected
UUID
Symbols
Shift+DCA
Password Policy Name
Keyboard Shortcut
Two Factor Key
Authentication Code Config
Authentication Code Length
Authentication Code Time Step
Authentication Code Start Time
.fi
.PP
Field names and values that contain whitespace characters must be quoted.
For example:
.PP
.nf
--subset="Run Command"=="remmina -c rdp://${user}:${password}@${url}"
.fi
.SH TIME VALUES
Times are expected in Universal Time Coordinated (UTC) format: YYYY/MM/DD hh:mm:ss.
The current time may also be referenced using the keyword
.B now
\&.
.SH SECURITY
Passing
.B --passphrase
or
.B --passphrase2
on the command line can expose passphrases through shell history or process
inspection. The application notes that these options should be avoided when
possible; use the interactive passphrase prompt instead.
